% Title
\title{\textbf{Distinct Clusters of Transcriptional Metabolic States in Single-Cell RNA-seq Data}}
\author{3CA Q1_R17 Analysis}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This study investigates whether transcriptional metabolic states can be used to identify distinct clusters in single-cell RNA-seq data. We analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773), using Reactome metabolism genes (R-HSA-1430728) for unsupervised clustering. The analysis employed Leiden clustering with multiple seeds and resolutions, compared against HVG and random gene-set controls, and assessed confounder effects. Results are inconclusive regarding discrete biological metabolic states, though metabolic genes do separate observed cell groups.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Metabolic reprogramming is a hallmark of cancer and other diseases. Single-cell RNA-seq has enabled the discovery of cell-type-specific metabolic programs, but whether these programs form discrete transcriptional states remains debated. This study asks: \textit{Can metabolic genes alone cluster or separate groups in single-cell RNA-seq data?}

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Dataset}
We used the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics experiment with 10 samples, 58,843 cells, and 33,538 genes. The dataset was downloaded from the 3CA catalog and verified with SHA-256 hash \texttt{51bf0452d166511fecca3f32d89f9fd0f631d173143173d82115f49cdaaa6c1d}.
