\end{table}

\subsection{Random Control Comparison}
None of the 19 random gene-set controls exceeded or matched the silhouette of the metabolic gene clustering. The add-one rank fraction was 0.05, which is a descriptive metric and not a biological P value.

\subsection{Confounder Assessment}
The NMI between clusters and categorical confounders was assessed:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Patient NMI: 0.551
    \item Sample NMI: 0.578
    \item Source NMI: 0.212
    \item Cell type NMI: 0.497
    \item Cell subtype NMI: 0.654
    \item Cell cycle phase NMI: 0.191
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Within-Replicate Sensitivity}
We assessed cluster stability within each patient (replicate). The global vs. within-replicate ARI ranged from 0.148 to 0.795, with patient 2 being the weakest replicate (914 cells).

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Metabolic Gene Separation}
Metabolic genes do separate observed cell groups in this dataset. The clustering produces 17 distinct clusters with reasonable silhouette scores. However, this separation does not necessarily imply discrete biological metabolic states.

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The analysis is exploratory and does not test discrete biological states against a continuum null model.
    \item Random controls are matched in size, not in expression mean or dispersion.
    \item The resolution was selected using the same dataset.
    \item No significance testing is performed on data-derived cluster labels.
    \item Confounder NMI thresholds do not establish independence.
\end{itemize}

