\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
This study investigates whether transcriptional metabolic states can be used to identify distinct clusters in single-cell RNA-seq data. We analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773), using Reactome metabolism genes (R-HSA-1430728) for unsupervised clustering. The analysis employed Leiden clustering with multiple seeds and resolutions, compared against HVG and random gene-set controls, and assessed confounder effects. Results are inconclusive regarding discrete biological metabolic states, though metabolic genes do separate observed cell groups.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Metabolic reprogramming is a hallmark of cancer and other diseases. Single-cell RNA-seq has enabled the discovery of cell-type-specific metabolic programs, but whether these programs form discrete transcriptional states remains debated. This study asks: \textit{Can metabolic genes alone cluster or separate groups in single-cell RNA-seq data?}

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Dataset}
We used the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics experiment with 10 samples, 58,843 cells, and 33,538 genes. The dataset was downloaded from the 3CA catalog and verified with SHA-256 hash \texttt{51bf0452d166511fecca3f32d89f9fd0f631d173143173d82115f49cdaaa6c1d}.

\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}
Reactome metabolism genes (R-HSA-1430728, release 97) were downloaded from \url{https://reactome.org}. The gene list contains 2,197 genes and was verified with SHA-256 hash \texttt{1e7f5adf240a04c95cb30fd2a8ef83bcab7d519f5f2d12447d8b3112b9589164}. After intersecting with the expression matrix, 1,984 metabolic genes were retained.

\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}
Raw UMI counts were normalized per cell by dividing by the library size and multiplying by 10,000, then log-transformed using the log1p function:
\begin{equation}
    x'_{ig} = \log\left(1 + \frac{10,000 \cdot x_{ig}}{\sum_g x_{ig}}\right)
\end{equation}
where $i$ indexes cells, $g$ indexes genes, $x_{ig}$ is the raw UMI count, and $x'_{ig}$ is the normalized expression. Features with $<500$ counts in $<20\%$ of cells were filtered out.

\subsection{Clustering}
Leiden clustering was performed on the PCA-reduced space (30 c
...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 6687 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...
e, and cell cycle phase using Normalized Mutual Information (NMI).

\section{Results}
\subsection{Clustering Performance}
Leiden clustering with the selected resolution (0.4) produced 17 clusters. The cluster size distribution ranged from 152 cells (cluster 15) to 9,017 cells (cluster 10). The silhouette coefficient was 0.216, with a median silhouette of 0.216 across seeds.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Clustering metrics and random control comparison}
\label{tab:metrics}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Silhouette} & \textbf{Calinski-Harabasz} & \textbf{Davies-Bouldin} & \textbf{Mean Pairwise ARI} \\
\midrule
Metabolic genes (Leiden) & 0.216 & 6,394.0 & 1.478 & 0.955 \\
HVG baseline & 0.288 & -- & -- & -- \\
Random controls (mean) & 0.221 & -- & -- & -- \\
Random controls (min) & 0.202 & -- & -- & -- \\
Random controls (max) & 0.235 & -- & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Random Control Comparison}
None of the 19 random gene-set controls exceeded or matched the silhouette of the metabolic gene clustering. The add-one rank fraction was 0.05, which is a descriptive metric and not a biological P value.

\subsection{Confounder Assessment}
The NMI between clusters and categorical confounders was assessed:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Patient NMI: 0.551
    \item Sample NMI: 0.578
    \item Source NMI: 0.212
    \item Cell type NMI: 0.497
    \item Cell subtype NMI: 0.654
    \item Cell cycle phase NMI: 0.191
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Within-Replicate Sensitivity}
We assessed cluster stability within each patient (replicate). The global vs. within-replicate ARI ranged from 0.148 to 0.795, with patient 2 being the weakest replicate (914 cells).

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Metabolic Gene Separation}
Metabolic genes do separate observed cell groups in this dataset. The clustering produces 17 distinct clusters with reasonable silhouette scores. However, this separation does not necessarily imply discrete biological metabolic states.

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The analysis is exploratory and does not test discrete biological states against a continuum null model.
    \item Random controls are matched in size, not in expression mean or dispersion.
    \item The resolution was selected using the same dataset.
    \item No significance testing is performed on data-derived cluster labels.
    \item Confounder NMI thresholds do not establish independence.
\end{itemize}

